576
00:48:13,920 --> 00:48:29,560
Because there are some countries right now, I won't announce anything, but I've spoken with some people that there's actually a trial going on about a countrywide bug bounty program that is being mandated by the government and funded in some capacity by the government.

577
00:48:29,560 --> 00:48:41,700
And sort of like what we were talking about before of how it's sort of, it's not a, the problem with like security as a feature makes it seem that whole phrase makes it seem like it's optional.

578
00:48:41,700 --> 00:48:57,620
Like, I feel like not putting money into the security program, more money into the, and the fact that you had 11 AppSec developers for this massive, you know, 130,000 person company is, is nothing beyond actual negligence.

579
00:48:58,420 --> 00:48:59,280
So I don't know.

580
00:48:59,340 --> 00:49:03,240
How would you guys feel about regulation when you mix your security person and your American?

581
00:49:03,900 --> 00:49:05,720
There's a really easy way to do this, by the way.

582
00:49:05,720 --> 00:49:07,280
That works with America.

583
00:49:07,700 --> 00:49:07,920
Okay.

584
00:49:07,920 --> 00:49:11,840
And it's how we do it right now, which is financial fines, right?

585
00:49:11,960 --> 00:49:19,120
So when, when, when a company gets breached, they're forced to pay settlements and they have to pay a fine because, you know, they were breached.

586
00:49:19,180 --> 00:49:20,160
And that happens right now.

587
00:49:20,200 --> 00:49:33,220
And I think that's currently sort of like the, basically addresses the negligence side, which is that there's a cost to negligence, which is that if you decide not to be secure and something happens, then you have to pay for that.

588
00:49:33,220 --> 00:49:38,280
But that being said, there are plenty of companies who make enough money where that doesn't matter.

589
00:49:38,540 --> 00:49:44,400
So I think that slapping them on the wrist is not really a good enough solution.

590
00:49:45,340 --> 00:49:53,140
Like, but it's, it would be very anti-American to put some sort of regulation like that in place.

591
00:49:53,520 --> 00:49:55,300
It wouldn't be novel though.

592
00:49:55,300 --> 00:50:02,180
So I think there are other things that exist that are like that, that could be used as sort of like some sort of like precedent.

593
00:50:02,180 --> 00:50:02,700
But yeah.

594
00:50:03,320 --> 00:50:09,740
I almost hope that like the SEC, which has some teeth in this space, comes out and starts hitting people a little harder.

595
00:50:09,860 --> 00:50:15,900
I mean, like we saw some of the cases that they brought against, you know, CISOs, for example, for, I think it was SolarWinds, right?

596
00:50:15,900 --> 00:50:22,000
And signing off on the security of their product only to get wrecked by Russians and then now being brought on charges, right?

597
00:50:22,240 --> 00:50:28,980
And by the way, like if you're, even in the C-suite, like if you're charged by the SEC for these things, even if you don't go to prison, you just have to pay a fine.

598
00:50:29,060 --> 00:50:34,720
Like you can no longer be an executive on any company's like board or leadership team ever again.

599
00:50:34,840 --> 00:50:35,760
This is like people's careers.

600
00:50:35,760 --> 00:50:39,940
And, you know, in some respects, I think that that's a good thing, right?

601
00:50:39,940 --> 00:50:48,940
Because then it does force them to pay a little bit more attention to the things that they're signing off on, which means in turn that in, you know, in theory, they should be then investing in the security that they should be investing in.

602
00:50:49,080 --> 00:50:53,580
And to come back to like the security as a feature thing, like the reason I say it that way is because features get invested in.

603
00:50:53,980 --> 00:50:58,040
And it's like, it's a required feature, not an optional feature is maybe what I mean by that.

604
00:50:58,300 --> 00:51:01,100
But so few companies do treat it as an optional feature.

605
00:51:01,100 --> 00:51:10,340
I mean, as we see with all the bugs that we find out in the world when we're hacking on things, it's like, yeah, clearly they didn't treat security as a feature because it's optional to them because they didn't invest in it.

606
00:51:10,360 --> 00:51:11,740
They're not investing in their security program.

607
00:51:11,740 --> 00:51:13,900
And we found this bug and hopefully we're getting paid for it.

608
00:51:13,900 --> 00:51:24,360
Do you consider like serving a website over HTTP versus like over SSH or something or Telnet?

609
00:51:24,720 --> 00:51:25,900
Do you consider that a feature?

610
00:51:26,140 --> 00:51:29,340
Wait, what do you mean serving it over Telnet?

611
00:51:29,340 --> 00:51:42,960
Like, let's say like if I had to access HackerOne over port 21 or something like versus the fact that I could access it over HTTP, is that like a feature or is that a core fundamental part of the product?

612
00:51:43,880 --> 00:51:44,100
Yeah.

613
00:51:44,200 --> 00:51:49,900
So like if you think about it, right, it almost always depends on the data that you're gathering or that you're providing to the website.

614
00:51:49,900 --> 00:52:00,900
Like secure like HTTPS today is like, again, we're living in a world where like speed of interfacing with applications and compute is fairly accessible.

615
00:52:00,900 --> 00:52:21,800
There were times probably about a decade, decade and a half ago where like deciding to serve a website over like TLS 1.1 was a decision that you had to make because your web server might not be able to handle all of the calls that are being made into the website and the cryptographic compute that is required to create secure connections.

616
00:52:21,800 --> 00:52:24,140
Like today, it's like, oh yeah, it's trivial.

617
00:52:24,240 --> 00:52:28,940
You should always be doing it, you know, HTTPS over TLS 1.3 or whatever version we're on now.

618
00:52:29,220 --> 00:52:30,320
So why isn't that a feature?

619
00:52:30,460 --> 00:52:30,580
Ten years.

620
00:52:31,660 --> 00:52:32,600
Or is it a feature?

621
00:52:33,120 --> 00:52:34,540
It is a feature now, right?

622
00:52:34,540 --> 00:52:37,640
Like it's a feature that I think it's universally something that people use.

623
00:52:37,840 --> 00:52:44,760
But 10, 15 years ago, it was definitely a feature you chose to invest in or not because it had compute costs associated with it, right?

624
00:52:44,780 --> 00:52:51,860
And so I think over time, these things, the thing that I've continued to find in my career is the pace of security.

625
00:52:51,860 --> 00:52:53,380
It has been 15 years since 2009 though.

626
00:52:54,100 --> 00:52:54,920
I know, right?

627
00:52:55,280 --> 00:52:57,980
Which is when you said that most AppSec teams started up, right?

628
00:52:57,980 --> 00:53:06,100
So I think the point I'm trying to get at here, sorry to cut you off, is that like, I think like we're getting to a point where security should not be a feature, right?

629
00:53:06,140 --> 00:53:17,980
Like security, if we consider TLS not to be a feature and to be a default functionality, like of like, it should be assumed that web servers serve on, you know, HTTP and HTTPS.

630
00:53:17,980 --> 00:53:29,400
It should be assumed that a company has some level of a security presence, even if that's one person or just like a point of contact who handles security related matters, who's on engineering.

631
00:53:30,180 --> 00:53:35,040
Like what is, you know, how much longer, right?

632
00:53:35,600 --> 00:53:37,600
You know, if it is a feature, right?

633
00:53:37,640 --> 00:53:39,460
Then like, when does it not become a feature?

634
00:53:40,360 --> 00:53:44,080
Yeah, I think, you know, to the point that you try to make security as transparent as possible.

635
00:53:44,080 --> 00:53:49,360
Like, like it's something you never have to think about because it's always just there and it's being done, right?

636
00:53:49,380 --> 00:53:56,200
Like that's the goal, I think, of any security program at the end of the day is like, you just want to be something that is done by default because you don't have to think of it anymore.

637
00:53:56,680 --> 00:53:58,540
Just like, you know, HTTPS, right?

638
00:53:58,600 --> 00:53:59,940
It's same concept.

639
00:54:01,540 --> 00:54:07,940
I've lost my train of thought here other than to say like, over time, I think the pace of security, though, and I've seen it over my career.

640
00:54:07,940 --> 00:54:11,980
I remember back in 2018, I was at a party with Jason Haddix at RSA.

641
00:54:11,980 --> 00:54:19,580
We were riding back in an Uber to our hotel and he's like, dude, you've got to get into JavaScript and like learning the JavaScript stuff.

642
00:54:19,600 --> 00:54:26,060
And I'm like, nah, man, I'm going to stick with like the PHP and the Java stuff because like there's plenty of bugs out there.

643
00:54:26,060 --> 00:54:34,100
Like, and Jason being the, you know, the Oracle that he is, like, and my sensei was absolutely right.

644
00:54:34,180 --> 00:54:41,920
And I look at this now, you know, six years later and I'm like, I listen to your podcast and I'm hearing all these amazing JavaScript bugs that people are finding.

645
00:54:41,980 --> 00:54:43,520
And I'm like, I should have listened to Jason.

646
00:54:43,520 --> 00:54:47,880
But I think the pace of security is accelerating.

647
00:54:48,060 --> 00:54:49,260
It continues to accelerate.

648
00:54:49,700 --> 00:54:54,700
Whereas the pace of development and the investments that companies make is just not right.

649
00:54:54,720 --> 00:54:58,600
Like they're, they're just barely keeping the doors open in many cases.

650
00:54:59,320 --> 00:55:08,760
And, and so I want to sort of round out this conversation here on, on VDPs by, by saying like, I mean, clearly in this scenario, you're working at a very big company.

651
00:55:08,760 --> 00:55:19,480
That very big company had a VDP and, and it almost makes me think of the customer service rep and the, the, the, the customer yelling at the customer service rep, right?

652
00:55:19,500 --> 00:55:31,080
The customer service rep doesn't have, you know, Keith can't just, you know, barge into finance's office and be like, listen, finance, like I need 30 people tomorrow or else we're going to get sued, you know?

653
00:55:31,080 --> 00:55:41,320
And so at the end of the day, like for, for me, I think it's less of a debate about like whether this company should be running a bug bounty program or whether it should be running a VDP or whatever it is.

654
00:55:41,320 --> 00:55:44,300
And more of like, okay, you try to work with what you've got in your scenario.

655
00:55:45,020 --> 00:56:01,020
And I think in many other companies scenarios, but I think whether we implement some sort of GDPR type thing, you know, like Europe's got where it's like, really, if you screw up in, in, you know, the cybersecurity realm in a negligent way, and you're found to be negligent.

656
00:56:01,360 --> 00:56:03,980
It's not like a pay a fine sort of thing.

657
00:56:04,020 --> 00:56:06,300
It's like a, Oh, you're out of business.

658
00:56:06,360 --> 00:56:08,200
You're bankrupt now because like.

659
00:56:08,280 --> 00:56:09,740
Percent of gross profits, right?

660
00:56:09,740 --> 00:56:13,040
Like that's the GDPR hit is like, it's not net profits.

661
00:56:13,140 --> 00:56:13,860
It's gross profits.

662
00:56:13,940 --> 00:56:15,900
It's your total revenue coming into the company.

663
00:56:16,020 --> 00:56:18,200
And if you had fines that were based off of that.

664
00:56:18,520 --> 00:56:18,920
Yeah.

665
00:56:18,960 --> 00:56:22,080
I think you'd start to see more of an investment in security overall.

666
00:56:23,580 --> 00:56:29,200
The things I've seen, like cannot be shared on the podcast of the various security vulnerabilities I've run into in my career.

667
00:56:29,200 --> 00:56:35,060
Not only like from my role at GitHub talking with customers, but also, you know, having led an appseq program myself.

668
00:56:35,380 --> 00:56:45,020
And I mean, I think you all, and especially your audience know, like the world is a lot less secure than we all seem to assume it is.

669
00:56:45,020 --> 00:56:46,460
But very few people actually know that.

670
00:56:46,500 --> 00:56:52,560
And we sort of feel like those people that are, you know, in front of the board with all the string attached being like crazy, like it's vulnerable.

671
00:56:52,560 --> 00:56:57,840
And it's like, nobody cares out there because their business is continuing to operate.

672
00:56:57,840 --> 00:57:05,520
And they're like, why would I pay, you know, millions of dollars in running a security program when I continue to operate just fine?

673
00:57:05,620 --> 00:57:09,460
And, you know, I have no service outages and I make all this revenue, like whatever.

