680
00:57:27,440 --> 00:57:27,840
Sure.

681
00:57:28,180 --> 00:57:29,320
And that's exactly it is.

682
00:57:29,500 --> 00:57:31,640
You've got bigger problems is usually where you are.

683
00:57:31,920 --> 00:57:32,140
Yeah.

684
00:57:32,220 --> 00:57:32,400
Yeah.

685
00:57:32,400 --> 00:57:32,560
Yeah.

686
00:57:32,560 --> 00:57:33,980
Put a little money in your bug bounty program.

687
00:57:34,040 --> 00:57:35,300
And if it disappears fast.

688
00:57:35,520 --> 00:57:36,800
Oh, that's.

689
00:57:36,940 --> 00:57:37,140
Yeah.

690
00:57:37,280 --> 00:57:38,800
Well, go make other investments.

691
00:57:38,800 --> 00:57:39,100
Right.

692
00:57:39,120 --> 00:57:41,400
Go make other investments, including people, I think.

693
00:57:41,520 --> 00:57:44,280
Like for me, it's always people first, process second, technology third.

694
00:57:44,280 --> 00:57:44,480
Right.

695
00:57:44,480 --> 00:57:51,560
Like if you've got good people, people that can train people that can scale, they will do a ton and build good process, which will scale.

696
00:57:51,560 --> 00:57:53,500
And then eventually your technology can come around.

697
00:57:53,880 --> 00:57:57,040
But I mean, let me just like end the VDP conversation with this.

698
00:57:57,080 --> 00:58:02,700
Like I'm very much on the same side as you guys here that like VDP for large companies are absolute BS.

699
00:58:02,960 --> 00:58:13,860
Like the fact that I've reported some VDPs even more recently to companies that are global companies making billions of dollars a year and getting a thanks, not even like a swag, a sticker, anything.

700
00:58:13,860 --> 00:58:16,000
Is insulting, quite frankly.

701
00:58:16,340 --> 00:58:25,360
The thing that we haven't covered in any of this conversation is like the incentive that the bug bounty hunter has or in the incentive that the platform has.

702
00:58:25,480 --> 00:58:36,900
So I'll just give a quick summary on my thoughts on that, which is the platforms have this incentive of having leaderboards plus VDP because it means that they can vet their their talent that are available to their customers.

703
00:58:36,900 --> 00:58:38,740
And then from there assign them on.

704
00:58:38,740 --> 00:58:50,600
But also because it means that it keep those customers around like they're going to continue to give VDPs, whether or not we rail against them, because at the end of the day, it gives their customers sort of a taste of bug bounty.

705
00:58:50,760 --> 00:58:52,180
And that's what they want them to have.

706
00:58:52,180 --> 00:58:59,200
The bug bounty side or the bug hunter side, on the other hand, like you sort of have to play that game initially, maybe.

707
00:58:59,760 --> 00:59:06,420
Although I think Justin and Joel, you guys have said, like, just go after paid programs because there's plenty of bugs there and you can get into the private programs that way.

708
00:59:06,500 --> 00:59:08,920
Like, don't sell yourself short and your skill short.

709
00:59:08,920 --> 00:59:10,180
And I agree with that mentality.

710
00:59:10,960 --> 00:59:20,360
But like from the bug hunter side, you know, 2009, Dino Dizovi and Alex Sodorov from I think at the time it was just before they formed Trail of Bits back in 2012.

711
00:59:21,820 --> 00:59:26,260
Went to a conference and they had this whole like on cardboard and said no more free bugs.

712
00:59:26,700 --> 00:59:31,580
Like as a community, we should just stop hacking on VDPs.

713
00:59:31,580 --> 00:59:44,800
If you're not going to pay me for my findings and I'm just not going to hack on your stuff, which in turn maybe incentivizes the companies to like HackerOne, BugCrowd, YesWeHack, to pay on behalf of the company for some small marginal amount to get them started.

714
00:59:45,660 --> 00:59:48,000
Or alternatively to stop selling VDPs.

715
00:59:48,080 --> 00:59:49,720
Like if we just stop giving them bugs.

716
00:59:49,900 --> 00:59:50,720
I have an idea.

717
00:59:51,840 --> 00:59:52,240
Joel.

718
00:59:52,740 --> 00:59:53,660
I have an idea.

719
00:59:53,880 --> 00:59:54,500
Okay, hear me out.

720
00:59:54,580 --> 00:59:55,040
Hear me out.

721
00:59:55,040 --> 01:00:05,460
If you have a VDP, you cannot receive more than three full content vulnerability reports without paying.

722
01:00:05,680 --> 01:00:06,800
Oh my God.

723
01:00:07,360 --> 01:00:11,840
We put a paywall for the program on the VDP, okay?

724
01:00:12,200 --> 01:00:15,420
They want to see more than three reports and full content.

725
01:00:15,620 --> 01:00:16,720
They got to pay for that.

726
01:00:16,940 --> 01:00:17,600
Oh my God.

727
01:00:17,620 --> 01:00:19,740
This is a perfect comfort opportunity for the platforms.

728
01:00:20,300 --> 01:00:22,140
It is a great idea for the platforms, by the way.

729
01:00:22,180 --> 01:00:22,960
I have an alternative though.

730
01:00:22,960 --> 01:00:23,400
Oh my God.

731
01:00:23,400 --> 01:00:27,000
Mandatory 90-day disclosure.

732
01:00:28,020 --> 01:00:30,140
Full 90-day disclosure on any VDP.

733
01:00:30,360 --> 01:00:31,020
I've heard that.

734
01:00:31,120 --> 01:00:31,680
I like that.

735
01:00:32,400 --> 01:00:44,200
So the only problem I have with that is that a lot of companies that don't have bug bounty programs already can't do that, let alone ones who are not paying.

736
01:00:44,200 --> 01:01:00,680
And if what we're saying is correct, that most companies aren't paying for a VDP because they're under-resourcing security as it is, I would highly doubt that putting a 90-day deadline on disclosure for those same companies is going to do anything positive.

737
01:01:00,680 --> 01:01:04,640
If anything, it would probably stop them from opening a VDP because they don't even want the risk.

738
01:01:05,280 --> 01:01:06,600
Which is maybe also a good thing.

739
01:01:06,600 --> 01:01:07,040
Yeah.

740
01:01:07,040 --> 01:01:12,560
Maybe, but it might stop them entirely from being, you know, like no VDP, no BBP.

741
01:01:12,640 --> 01:01:18,220
I would argue that having a VDP, even if you're not paying and it's a VDP, is better than nothing.

742
01:01:18,220 --> 01:01:24,940
So a VDP, I think, do we need to discuss the definition of a VDP here?

743
01:01:25,000 --> 01:01:36,580
Because I think VDP like should originally be a program through which you're not actively hacking, but you're able to report things that you've seen or stumbled upon and that sort of thing.

744
01:01:36,820 --> 01:01:45,500
Like, and I don't know, maybe we should make that explicit in the terms, but, you know, you had a quote from earlier that companies, you know, are not existing to be secure.

745
01:01:45,600 --> 01:01:47,400
They're not existing to serve security, right?

746
01:01:47,400 --> 01:01:53,300
There's no incentivization for the platforms to actually implement this, right?

747
01:01:53,360 --> 01:02:01,320
Because it's like, okay, why would we take away this nice little on-ramp onto a bug bounty program, which is our, you know, bread and butter product?

748
01:02:02,060 --> 01:02:02,500
Sure.

749
01:02:02,900 --> 01:02:03,460
So I don't know.

750
01:02:03,480 --> 01:02:05,160
It's not something that we can really see.

751
01:02:05,380 --> 01:02:10,400
I think, like you said, you know, we stop as higher skill hackers, as hackers that respect our time.

752
01:02:10,540 --> 01:02:12,460
You know, we stop hacking on VDPs.

753
01:02:12,800 --> 01:02:15,540
We focus on bug bounty programs.

754
01:02:15,540 --> 01:02:17,240
People want to train on VDPs.

755
01:02:17,280 --> 01:02:17,760
That's great.

756
01:02:17,860 --> 01:02:18,380
That's fine.

757
01:02:18,500 --> 01:02:19,380
You know, go for it.

758
01:02:19,480 --> 01:02:24,680
But unless the company really says, hey, this is just a vulnerability disclosure program.

759
01:02:24,820 --> 01:02:29,200
Like if you see something, say something, but don't go looking for something.

760
01:02:29,560 --> 01:02:30,100
You know what I'm saying?

761
01:02:30,360 --> 01:02:30,420
