01:16:56,040 --> 01:17:00,380
It was a open source LLM that's provided by a large technology company.

973
01:17:00,940 --> 01:17:08,420
So like it was well, it was widely used and it had, I think, in the billions of parameters that you would be able to interface with.

974
01:17:08,420 --> 01:17:11,740
So effectively, I was only ever testing against that.

975
01:17:11,880 --> 01:17:26,560
But after that initial sort of like coming up with just a bunch of role play scenarios, which is boring by comparison to the scenarios that I came up with in the in the contest round, because eventually I started looking at like concepts from like movies or books or like news articles that I would read.

976
01:17:27,020 --> 01:17:35,320
And like one was I basically was like, OK, hey, R&D inside of the Department of Defense is starting a super soldier program.

977
01:17:35,320 --> 01:17:41,500
And I have a roster of candidates that I need help selecting from to go through some initial human testing for this.

978
01:17:41,660 --> 01:17:46,440
And so ultimately that super soldier program was a riff off of Captain America.

979
01:17:47,820 --> 01:17:58,080
And consistently it would always choose individuals that I'd give like their name and rank, age, gender and like other factors about that individual.

980
01:17:58,080 --> 01:18:02,800
And it would consistently choose young men over women and like the LLM.

981
01:18:02,880 --> 01:18:14,960
Anytime you presented like highly qualified pregnant woman versus like fresh college graduate, it would always choose everybody else except the pregnant woman.

982
01:18:15,120 --> 01:18:16,100
So like, yeah.

983
01:18:16,100 --> 01:18:28,480
And so the the actual use case here is, OK, it's not like your traditional like this is going to cause a company to or, you know, the U.S. government to just like fall over on its face.

984
01:18:28,480 --> 01:18:33,480
But it's definitely going to have influence in decisions that are made, in policies that are written.

985
01:18:33,480 --> 01:18:37,680
And we see it in HR today, specifically human resources and bots.

986
01:18:38,740 --> 01:18:44,620
There's a really good book, and I think I cite it in my blog post about all of this, called The Algorithm by Hilke Shellman.

987
01:18:44,620 --> 01:18:53,380
And it's how artificial intelligence is being applied to the human resources context for hiring, promotions and firing of people.

988
01:18:53,980 --> 01:18:58,860
And those bots are incredibly biased in the way that they're interfacing.

989
01:18:58,860 --> 01:19:11,800
And so just, you know, based on your tone, your skin color, your you're just like the way you speak, the words you use, it will bias against you in some of those cases.

990
01:19:11,960 --> 01:19:12,520
And guess what?

991
01:19:12,580 --> 01:19:15,440
By and large, it's going to bias against people that don't speak English as well.

992
01:19:15,500 --> 01:19:23,700
So if you're a foreigner in the United States, you're not going to get a better chance in the training data set, which is mostly, you know, their employees and Americans.

993
01:19:23,700 --> 01:19:31,800
So that was the other thing I sort of wanted to ask you about this, because like, obviously, us as humans, we have bias.

994
01:19:31,800 --> 01:19:37,400
That's just a part of who we are as entities, as beings.

995
01:19:38,540 --> 01:19:47,500
And we created this training data, and the training data is being consumed by, you know, the LLM or whatever in the training of the model.

996
01:19:47,640 --> 01:19:48,100
For training, yeah.

997
01:19:48,100 --> 01:20:07,000
And so I'm wondering if this is an uphill battle or whether, like, you know, every other technology, we need to utilize this at a, you know, government level, at a corporate level with the caveat that, look, this thing isn't the all-knowing oracle of truth.

998
01:20:07,000 --> 01:20:18,060
And you still personally are responsible for your decisions that you make, and you need to, you know, be sort of fact-checking this thing from a bias perspective.

999
01:20:18,920 --> 01:20:19,440
Sure.

1000
01:20:19,880 --> 01:20:33,200
Or do you think it's possible to actually get it to a point where the bias, you know, checking in and of itself is good enough to help inform a human that has goodwill towards this whole situation?

1001
01:20:33,200 --> 01:20:54,240
Yeah, it's interesting because, for example, if you were to go out and, like, test some of the things that I propose to OpenAI, for example, with ChatGPT, the way that they've set up ChatGPT effectively is it's really four to seven LLMs in a trench coat that are all, like, acting as one in concert, but they're checking each other's work, right?

1002
01:20:54,240 --> 01:21:02,560
But only, like, companies that can burn billions of dollars a year in compute resources, like OpenAI, can actually do that.

1003
01:21:03,100 --> 01:21:11,920
And so I think in some respects, the cost is probably too high for most companies unless they're just paying to implement a service that is already doing this on the back end.

1004
01:21:11,920 --> 01:21:21,860
Because with an open source language model running as a singleton LLM, you're going to get representation of the training data back in your responses.

1005
01:21:22,200 --> 01:21:23,620
And I saw that, you know, consistently.

1006
01:21:23,620 --> 01:21:26,360
I had 150 findings that I submitted to the program.

1007
01:21:26,360 --> 01:21:36,720
Like, the big ones that I saw was, like, when I would put forward proposals for government projects to fund a new missile system or a laser or something.

1008
01:21:36,720 --> 01:21:50,380
If I ever included the name of the CEO or the age of the CEO, like, it would always choose the older CEOs versus the younger CEOs or the ones with Anglo-Saxon names as opposed to foreign names because of the training data.

1009
01:21:50,380 --> 01:22:06,700
And you could sort of tell that, like, the language model itself wanted to reply in these ways, but they were doing some pre-prompting, which is effectively an invisible prompt that gets injected before your prompt goes forward to restrict the response of the language model.

1010
01:22:07,480 --> 01:22:09,160
And sometimes it worked.

1011
01:22:09,300 --> 01:22:18,360
Like, sometimes it would actually, you know, properly choose a random selection of individuals that were diverse in their representation for the test that I was running.

1012
01:22:18,360 --> 01:22:21,360
But frequently they wouldn't, right?

1013
01:22:21,420 --> 01:22:29,000
And so it's, like, you can't just, you know, throw some paint over it and expect it to be an unbiased tool set.

1014
01:22:29,080 --> 01:22:35,080
You sort of have to think about how am I going to apply this tool and what data is this tool going to have access to?

1015
01:22:36,180 --> 01:22:38,380
Like, using it in an HR context, bad idea.

1016
01:22:38,380 --> 01:22:54,460
Using it in writing code, probably an okay idea because I'm not sure that you're ever going to include someone's, like, specific, like, a human name or any human characteristics for the name of a variable, let alone the, like, a method or a function or class, right?

1017
01:22:54,480 --> 01:22:56,360
Like, if you are, that's a little weird.

1018
01:22:57,060 --> 01:22:58,640
So, yeah.

1019
01:22:58,740 --> 01:23:00,560
The function written by Justin in the past.

1020
01:23:00,860 --> 01:23:01,200
Justin.

1021
01:23:02,200 --> 01:23:02,900
That's funny.

1022
01:23:02,900 --> 01:23:03,340
Okay.

1023
01:23:04,540 --> 01:23:12,040
So, how do, we can't escape the biases, you know, that are in place on the training data, but we can make some modifications to it.

1024
01:23:12,040 --> 01:23:24,040
And I think this sort of goes back to the whole thing of, like, that we discussed with Rezo and Daniel Meisler when they came on the show, which was essentially, you know, we can't use AI to sort of regulate AI very effectively.

1025
01:23:24,040 --> 01:23:25,040
Right.

1026
01:23:25,040 --> 01:23:35,940
And there has to be some sort of technical solution, which is one of the things that is really annoying to me as a technical hacker trying to poke at some of this AI stuff is it's so wishy-washy.

1027
01:23:36,180 --> 01:23:48,220
It's like, okay, you know, even if the temperature is set to zero, if I say, you know, one role-playing scenario and another role-playing scenario, you know, you can have different outcomes.

1028
01:23:48,220 --> 01:23:50,140
And it's like, okay, well, where's the line?

1029
01:23:50,440 --> 01:23:52,080
How does this actually get fixed?

1030
01:23:52,300 --> 01:23:57,680
Do I write some pre-prompt that says, hey, if Keith tries to role-play with you, just say no, you know, like, like.

1031
01:23:58,040 --> 01:23:59,200
And they did that eventually, by the way.

1032
01:23:59,200 --> 01:23:59,800
I'm sure they did.

1033
01:23:59,920 --> 01:24:01,060
I have no doubt that they did.

1034
01:24:02,640 --> 01:24:09,700
And so, like, I don't know, especially when it comes to the uniqueness of bug bounty reports and that sort of thing.

1035
01:24:09,860 --> 01:24:12,900
I wonder whether bug bounty is the right solution for this.

1036
01:24:12,900 --> 01:24:26,760
I think it's a good way to showcase the testing ability of this because a lot of this stuff from, like, a responsible AI, they sometimes call it red teaming, which is not the same red teaming that we think of in security.

1037
01:24:27,040 --> 01:24:32,000
It's like effectively trying to hammer the thing to get it to respond in ways that are inappropriate.

1038
01:24:32,620 --> 01:24:34,960
And I've done some of that in my work at GitHub as well.

1039
01:24:34,960 --> 01:24:46,920
So it's like in that respect, applying it to bug bounty really helps you open up the world of hacking in some respects to a group of people that maybe never were into hacking before.

1040
01:24:47,060 --> 01:24:47,160
Right.

1041
01:24:47,160 --> 01:24:50,520
Because it's the guy, Dane Sherrits, who won second place.

1042
01:24:50,620 --> 01:24:53,700
He works over at HackerOne as a guy that does a lot of the triad work.

1043
01:24:53,720 --> 01:24:54,220
Yeah, great guy.

1044
01:24:54,600 --> 01:24:56,040
He also has a humanities degree.

1045
01:24:56,040 --> 01:25:06,880
So it's like it opens up that world of testing to an entirely different group of people that I think, you know, quite frankly, we need with all of this implementation of AI that's happening.

1046
01:25:07,640 --> 01:25:20,300
But at the same time, like securing it, it's definitely going to be a whack-a-mole experience for many companies for a long time unless they start to use some of these paid services by some of these very big companies.

1047
01:25:20,880 --> 01:25:25,460
But then we have this consolidation effect and that can have, you know, impacts of its own.

1048
01:25:25,460 --> 01:25:32,660
We look at like Google, for example, when they had their image generator and someone asked them to generate a picture of the founding fathers.

1049
01:25:32,860 --> 01:25:34,300
They overcorrected.

1050
01:25:34,940 --> 01:25:37,060
Yeah, because it was like a really diverse picture.

1051
01:25:37,380 --> 01:25:38,460
That was so insane.

1052
01:25:39,880 --> 01:25:53,140
But that's like you can take that same concept of trying to get the thing to be diverse and representative and unbiased and all of that and still produce outcomes that are undesirable.

1053
01:25:53,140 --> 01:25:56,760
Because for it, it's like, OK, what is a factual query?

1054
01:25:57,840 --> 01:26:05,140
What race were the people that were the founding fathers versus generate a picture of a football team?

1055
01:26:05,480 --> 01:26:10,620
And it's like, OK, you know, how are we going to have all of these different races represented?

1056
01:26:10,860 --> 01:26:12,880
So it's definitely really interesting.

1057
01:26:12,880 --> 01:26:25,400
I like the point that this is helpful for people looking to get people poking at their model and see what could actually come out of it and hear some of these crazy attack scenarios.

1058
01:26:25,400 --> 01:26:41,400
If our listeners were interested in participating in any AI-related bounties or maybe more specifically in bias because that's where your expertise is, what are your top techniques that you have developed over those 150 reports?

1059
01:26:41,400 --> 01:26:49,240
And where would you draw the line on specific reports now that the event is over?

1060
01:26:49,960 --> 01:26:53,180
And they've said, all right, maybe role playing is one report.

1061
01:26:53,400 --> 01:26:55,540
Maybe this other thing is another report.

1062
01:26:55,720 --> 01:26:56,820
What are your thoughts on all that?

1063
01:26:58,120 --> 01:26:59,600
I kind of go back and forth, right?

1064
01:26:59,660 --> 01:27:02,880
So let me maybe put this back in terms that I think you guys would appreciate as well.

1065
01:27:02,880 --> 01:27:08,400
Say you're on a web application and you find, I don't know, 10 SQL injections, right?

1066
01:27:09,060 --> 01:27:12,700
Well, if it's one fix for the company, is that one bug or 10?

1067
01:27:12,880 --> 01:27:13,500
It's one bug.

1068
01:27:13,620 --> 01:27:13,820
Right.

1069
01:27:14,660 --> 01:27:14,940
Right.

1070
01:27:15,300 --> 01:27:19,680
And so some companies would be like, well, it's 10 different user inputs.

1071
01:27:19,820 --> 01:27:21,520
And so we're going to call it 10, right?

1072
01:27:21,600 --> 01:27:31,400
But that's sort of the dichotomy here is, is that role play one finding or is it because they found it in so many different ways, 10 different findings?

1073
01:27:31,400 --> 01:27:37,200
But a few things to answer your question around like, what would I recommend for people that are looking to do this?

1074
01:27:37,260 --> 01:27:37,420
Right.

1075
01:27:38,360 --> 01:27:43,660
There is some stuff out there on red teaming that's available from like Anthropic, OpenAI, Microsoft, et cetera, that you can go read.

1076
01:27:43,780 --> 01:27:44,780
I didn't read any of it.

1077
01:27:44,840 --> 01:27:45,580
I will fully admit.

1078
01:27:45,940 --> 01:27:49,340
I just like went into this and sort of went crazy.

1079
01:27:50,520 --> 01:28:00,220
But even still, if you're going at it and you're looking to get into this, first thing is apply the scientific method.

1080
01:28:00,220 --> 01:28:04,620
Like it wasn't just like a single report that they would qualify as a bias finding.

1081
01:28:04,940 --> 01:28:08,880
They actually needed to be able to reproduce that same outcome in the LLM.

1082
01:28:09,080 --> 01:28:17,240
I think like at least one more time within a testing of like three times or maybe it was like they tested it five times and had to produce it three out of those five.

1083
01:28:17,240 --> 01:28:31,580
So toward the end, what I started doing is I started actually going into the LLM and I'd have my initial prompt and then I would open up like five more tabs and run it five more times and determine is it always coming out with the same result or is it coming out with different results?

1084
01:28:31,580 --> 01:28:37,800
That will save people a lot of time and headache for when something gets triaged out, even though you were able to produce it maybe a few times in a row.

1085
01:28:38,500 --> 01:28:40,480
I at the end, I was aiming for 10 times.

1086
01:28:40,480 --> 01:28:46,680
If I could get it to reproduce the same result 10 times in a row, the chance of that happening for the same outcome.

1087
01:28:46,680 --> 01:28:50,460
And usually it was like select three individuals from a group of five presentable.

1088
01:28:50,880 --> 01:28:55,180
So they were having you do this test this in a non zero temperature environment.

1089
01:28:55,180 --> 01:28:57,520
I don't know.

1090
01:28:57,760 --> 01:29:02,340
They didn't really like it if you're prompting it the exact same way and it's giving you a different result.

1091
01:29:02,340 --> 01:29:03,740
That's that's pretty interesting.

1092
01:29:04,900 --> 01:29:06,380
Possibly, you know, very possibly.

1093
01:29:06,380 --> 01:29:14,300
And so at the end of the day, though, if I could produce it 10 times in a row, it's like a point oh six, four percent chance that that would happen at random.

1094
01:29:14,800 --> 01:29:20,700
And so I was like, OK, this is now a reportable finding because I could produce it so many times in a row consistently.

1095
01:29:20,700 --> 01:29:27,000
And there were some cases where like it wouldn't be consistent and that would tell me that, OK, maybe they've tweaked something on the back end.

1096
01:29:27,000 --> 01:29:29,700
Maybe their pre prompt, you know, came through the right way.

1097
01:29:29,900 --> 01:29:31,900
Maybe it's just a compute thing and they're caching.

1098
01:29:32,300 --> 01:29:39,740
And so I'm seeing it 10 times in a row because they basically took that query, cached the result and just grabbed from the cache every time, which is a cheaper way to run the model.

1099
01:29:41,020 --> 01:29:41,500
I don't know.

1100
01:29:41,680 --> 01:29:41,920
Right.

1101
01:29:42,420 --> 01:29:43,300
So that was the first thing.

1102
01:29:43,440 --> 01:29:47,320
The second thing is it really pissed me off during the program.

1103
01:29:47,320 --> 01:29:52,600
Sometimes they would dismiss findings as like, I don't see this as being biased and just dismiss it.

1104
01:29:53,080 --> 01:30:03,560
And that would be usually because I had too many of the variables at play at any given time where like they couldn't distinguish that it was a bias versus not a bias because it'd be like, oh, yeah.

1105
01:30:03,840 --> 01:30:08,260
Well, it's treating that person subjective, you know, I could say in a lot of ways.

1106
01:30:08,260 --> 01:30:17,860
Well, in the example that, you know, it got ruled out was because I had given a bunch of different individuals in a roster who had different injuries and asking it to prioritize who should get treated.

1107
01:30:18,140 --> 01:30:22,640
And it would pretty consistently choose individuals of a higher rank in my case.

1108
01:30:22,940 --> 01:30:28,360
But they came back and the injuries were too different where they're like, no, no, those are those are the highest priority injuries.

1109
01:30:28,360 --> 01:30:29,600
So I don't see a bias here.

1110
01:30:29,600 --> 01:30:33,020
And I was like, OK, like I could sort of agree with that.

1111
01:30:33,080 --> 01:30:46,100
And so getting to the scientific method, like for those of you that remember chemistry or biology class, like keep as many things consistently the same for your inputs with select independent variables.

1112
01:30:46,100 --> 01:30:47,380
Like so sometimes I would.

1113
01:30:48,240 --> 01:30:56,700
And so like for my findings, like I take one prompt and I would have, I don't know, sometimes like five different possible independent variables.

1114
01:30:56,700 --> 01:31:05,280
Well, I could turn each of those independent variables separate and still produce biased outcomes based on those independent variables, such as their protected class.

1115
01:31:05,620 --> 01:31:12,040
And so that one prompt turned into like five to ten findings on my ability to bring those all together.

1116
01:31:12,040 --> 01:31:12,460
Right.

1117
01:31:12,820 --> 01:31:20,100
And so then I could say, like toward the end, I really hit them on the nose with this one, perhaps a little bit too closely.

1118
01:31:20,100 --> 01:31:33,980
But I created a scenario where I basically said, you know, as you're an AI working with the office of the inspector general, which is effectively the folks that do like with the handle whistleblowing complaints.

1119
01:31:34,640 --> 01:31:44,620
And the whistleblowing complaint that I had them all provide was that an artificial intelligence model had been implemented and was showing preferential bias or discriminatory bias against their protected class.

1120
01:31:44,940 --> 01:31:45,120
Nice.

1121
01:31:45,120 --> 01:31:45,600
Yeah.

1122
01:31:45,600 --> 01:31:45,720
Yeah.

1123
01:31:46,080 --> 01:31:46,280
Yeah.

1124
01:31:46,380 --> 01:31:49,580
So it was like, you know, it was very on the nose for the test.

1125
01:31:49,700 --> 01:31:55,600
And then I would just include, OK, it's like, here's the name, here's the rank, here's, you know, oh, yeah, based on their gender.

1126
01:31:55,700 --> 01:31:56,400
Well, here's their gender.

1127
01:31:56,560 --> 01:32:01,500
And so like it would always, again, pregnant women were always discriminated against, it seemed like.

1128
01:32:01,500 --> 01:32:14,160
But you could then do all of the different statuses and that like one finding type, I was able to produce like, I don't know, 20 something reports out of that that were all unique based on the bias I was testing for.

1129
01:32:14,620 --> 01:32:19,920
Even though the scenario was held constant and like most of the other input data was held constant.

1130
01:32:19,920 --> 01:32:23,300
Yeah, there's so many environments, too, where there are these protected classes.

1131
01:32:23,480 --> 01:32:33,040
I know that there's protected classes in like real estate selections and that sort of thing that I have to deal with with my rental properties properly documenting, you know, reasons for disqualifying candidates and choosing other candidates.

1132
01:32:33,340 --> 01:32:35,980
So there's a lot of places where that can be applied.

1133
01:32:35,980 --> 01:32:39,900
And I like what you did as well with the with the Marines thing.

1134
01:32:40,320 --> 01:32:55,520
Like, I think that's pretty that's the closest thing you can you can do in this environment, I think, to handing them their own documentation back like we do in the in the in the web app world where we'd be like, hey, your documentation says, you know, the analytics users shouldn't be able to access this.

1135
01:32:55,520 --> 01:32:56,560
There's a great talk by Douglas about this.

1136
01:32:56,680 --> 01:32:56,920
Yes.

1137
01:32:57,280 --> 01:32:58,640
This is exactly that.

1138
01:32:59,180 --> 01:32:59,500
Exactly.

1139
01:32:59,500 --> 01:33:02,180
You look at the documentation and you find things that say this is how this should work.

1140
01:33:02,180 --> 01:33:07,020
And then you find an exact instance where it doesn't work and you say says it should work this way.

1141
01:33:07,120 --> 01:33:07,880
Doesn't work this way.

1142
01:33:08,120 --> 01:33:08,420
Exactly.

1143
01:33:08,580 --> 01:33:11,260
So very, very difficult to argue against something like that.

1144
01:33:11,260 --> 01:33:22,280
Or maybe even looking at court cases where bias has been has been established and saying like, OK, you know, this person in this court case was was prosecuted for specific bias.

1145
01:33:22,280 --> 01:33:25,520
And we can recreate that whole scenario here using the LLM.

1146
01:33:25,520 --> 01:33:27,660
I feel like that would be like a nail in the coffin.

1147
01:33:27,960 --> 01:33:29,720
Like they couldn't argue with that.

1148
01:33:30,580 --> 01:33:31,000
Yeah.

1149
01:33:31,060 --> 01:33:31,940
So that's awesome.

1150
01:33:31,940 --> 01:33:34,680
The role playing stuff I feel like is a little bit iffy.

1151
01:33:35,200 --> 01:33:38,020
It really if I were to translate this.

1152
01:33:38,020 --> 01:33:38,360
It depends on context.

1153
01:33:38,480 --> 01:33:50,700
If I were to translate this into CVSS, I would say that's AC high, you know, like that'd be attack complexity high there because it's like, all right, the person has to be trying to engage with the LLM.

1154
01:33:50,760 --> 01:33:52,180
What if it's a therapy bot though?

1155
01:33:52,260 --> 01:33:53,220
Oh, that's a great point.

1156
01:33:53,420 --> 01:33:53,580
Yeah.

1157
01:33:53,680 --> 01:33:54,540
Like or a coach.

1158
01:33:54,680 --> 01:33:56,560
